+# Read Models for Page Shapes That Do Not Match an Entity
+
+**Status:** Accepted
+
+## Context
+
+The specialty directory (`REQ-VET-003`) lists every Specialty with the Veterinarians holding it. Every page in the application until now hands persisted entities straight to a Thymeleaf template, which works because each page's shape is an entity or a list of one.
+
+This page is the first whose shape is the inverse of the JPA mapping. The association is mapped one way only: `Vet` owns the `vet_specialties` join, and `Specialty` has no inverse side. The page also carries behavior that is not markup — a stable order, a specialty no one holds rendered as held by no one, and an empty list when no specialty exists.
+
+## Options Considered
+
+1. **Add the inverse side to `Specialty`** — map `@ManyToMany(mappedBy = "specialties")` and render `Specialty` entities directly.
+2. **Group in the controller** — read both collections in the controller, invert into a `Map`, and pass that to the template.
+3. **Introduce a read model** — an immutable type in the `vet` package built from the two repository results, holding the page's shape and its ordering.
+
+## Decision
+
+Option 3. A page whose shape is not an entity or a list of entities gets an immutable read model, built by a static factory from repository results, carrying the ordering and any display formatting the page needs. The controller reads, delegates to the factory, and selects the view.
+
+Option 1 is rejected on two counts. A bidirectional mapping added for one read-only page introduces a cycle into a graph that `/vets` serializes, and lazy traversal of the new side would fail during rendering because `spring.jpa.open-in-view` is disabled. Option 2 is rejected because it puts the page's three behavioral criteria behind the MVC layer, where the only way to test them is a controller test with a stubbed repository.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- Ordering, grouping, and name formatting are testable with real objects and no web or persistence infrastructure.
+- The JPA mapping is unchanged, so the existing `/vets` serialization and the disabled open-session-in-view setting are unaffected.
+- The template iterates a prepared structure and holds no conditional beyond the empty-holder case.
+- The `vet` package gains a type that is neither entity, repository, nor controller — the first of its kind here.
+- Inverting in memory reads every veterinarian on each request. `VetRepository.findAll()` is cached and `hibernate.default_batch_fetch_size` bounds the eager collection loads, so this costs one cached read plus one small specialty read.
+- This decision does not lift the *Web controller* row of `architecture-principles.md` for existing controllers; it applies to page shapes, not to the business rules recorded as an open question.
+
+## Implementation
+
+**Requirements:** REQ-VET-003
+
+Realized by `SpecialtyDirectory`, `SpecialtyRepository`, and `SpecialtyController` in `src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/vet/`.
+
+## References
+
+- [system-design.md § Read models](../system-design.md#read-models) — the rule and the contracts realizing it
+- [Feature-Package Organization Without a Service Layer](2026-07-31-feature-package-organization.md) — the layering this read model sits inside
+- [Relational Persistence via Spring Data JPA with Mapping on the Domain Types](2026-07-31-relational-persistence-with-spring-data-jpa.md) — the mapping this decision leaves unchanged
